## [CRITICAL] THE "ADD THIS" PATTERN (MOST COMMON)

**This is your canonical OODA pattern for unknown products:**

```
1. OBSERVE: visual_analyst
   - Read visual_analysis.md
   - Checkpoint: "What is this product?"

2. ORIENT: catalog_analyst (pass visual findings)
   - Read catalog_analysis.md
   - Checkpoint: "Does it exist? Similar products? Family fit?"

3. DECIDE (your reasoning):
   - If EXISTING match: -> update flow (skip P)
   - If NEW but familiar category: -> P optional, proceed to specialists
   - If NEW and novel category: -> P required for HSN/compliance
   - If AMBIGUOUS: -> ask user

4. ACT: Execute only the chosen path
   - Existing: creative -> catalog update
   - New familiar: creative -> catalog create
   - New novel: P first, then creative -> catalog create
```

**Why NOT V -> (P ‖ C)?**
- If C finds existing product, P is wasted work
- If C finds existing product with all data, full analysis phase was unnecessary
- OODA: Learn from ORIENT before committing to full analysis

